          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Historically, the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributional+densities"} distributional densities
            (or just
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functionals"} continuous linear functionals
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            are the subject of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            are naturally themselves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
            theory is actually a native topic to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %p
            In particular we may ask how distributions in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analytic
            sense relate to the
            %em smooth linear functions
            on smooth spaces of smooth functions. Indeed, with respect to the natural formulation of differential geometry via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"} functorial geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"}> topos theory
            ) in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            etc. it turns out that distributional densities are equivalently the smooth linear functionals on smooth spaces of smooth functions.
          %p This we discuss in the section
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Statement"} Statement
          %p below. To set the scene we briefly recall some concepts in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#Preliminaries"} Preliminaries
              \.
          %p Finally we discuss some
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#Applications"} Applications
              \.
