                    \\tilde \mu(\Phi_{(-)})(u)
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\langle \mu, \Phi_{u}\rangle
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#MoerdijkReyes91"}> Moerdijk-Reyes 91, chapter II, prop. 3.6
            )
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              First consider this for the case that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothSet"} SmoothSet
              (which immediately subsumes the case that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} DiffelogicaSpace
            %p
              To see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{(-)}
              is well defined, we need to check that the function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mover
                                  %mi μ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mi Φ
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℝ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi u
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi μ
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi Φ
                              %mi u
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    U
                    \&amp;\overset{
                    \\tilde \mu(\Phi_{(-)})}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    \\mathbb{R}
                    \\\
                    u &amp;\mapsto&amp; \langle \mu, \Phi_u\rangle
                    }
            %p
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \mu,-\rangle
              by definition is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
            %p
              To see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{(-)}
              is indeed a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              it remains that every
              = succeed "-linear" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \;\colon\; [\mathbb{R}^n,\mathbb{R}] \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p
              when restricted on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+elements"} global elements
              to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              of sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A(\ast) \;\colon\; C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n) \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
              with respect to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
              structure from def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TVSOfCompactlySupportedFunctions"}
              on the left.
            %p
              Now by definition of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is actually “path-smooth” (
              %a{:href => "Frechet+space#PathSmoothFunction"}> this def.
              ) and hence the statement is implied by
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "Frechet+space#PathSmoothLinearFunctionsOnFrechetSpaceAreContinuous"} this prop
            %p
              Finally to see that this argument generalizes to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+smooth+set"} FormalSmoothSet
              observe that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
              of every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} infinitesimally thickened point
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+ring"} quotient ring
              of an algebra of smooth functions on some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              (by the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+lemma"} Hadamard lemma
          %h3#general_distributions General distributions
          %p
            For general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functionals"} continuous linear functionals
            on the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R}^n)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compactly supported
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+functions"}> bump functions
            ) their equivalence to smooth linear functionals is discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#KockReyes04"}> Kock-Reyes 04, section 2, esp. paragraph above theorem 2.3
            ).
          %p (…)
          %h2#Applications Applications
          %h3#in_quantum_field_theory In quantum field theory
          %p
            This has some interesting consequences. For instance a major application of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
            theory is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            is traditionally regarded to be a subject that makes heavy use of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            with its emphasis of the abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} of observables
            and de-emphasis of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of quantum states
            (which is sometimes a convenient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            of the abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            but more often just a distraction) combined with the development of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
            are replaced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+algebras"} star algebras
            of smooth functionals, has diminished the fundamental role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            in field theory. The theory of distributions survives as the tool of choice for understanding the singularity structure (or rather its absence) in the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
            and then in the process of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            to the locus of coincident interaction points. But the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            which are represented by these distributions are most naturally thought of as
            %em smooth
            functions on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is a tool for studying smooth functionals, not a concept fundamental to the theory as such.
          %h3#generalization_to_extensive_quantities Generalization to extensive quantities
          %p
            The conception of distributions simply as elements of a double
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"}> internal hom
            ) makes immediate that this concept exists in more generality than the traditional context. In
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere86"} Lawvere 86
            it was suggested that distribution theory should be considered more abstractly from such a point of general “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+quantities"}> extensive quantities
            ”. This is developed further in (
            %a{:href => "#Kock11"}> Kock 11
            ).
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Discussion of distributions in terms morphisms out of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+homs"} internal homs
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            over the site of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+loci"} smooth loci
            is in
          %ul
            %li#MoerdijkReyes91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
          %p
            Discussion for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cahiers+topos"} Cahiers topos
            is in
          %ul
            %li#KockReyes03
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some calculus with extensive quantities: wave equation
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/11/14/11-14abs.html"} TAC
            %li#KockReyes04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                %em Categorical distribution theory; heat equation
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0407242"}> arXiv:math/0407242
                )
            %li#Kock11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                %em Commutative monads as a theory of distributions
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1108.5952"}> arxiv/1108.5952
                )
          %p using results of
          %ul
            %li#FroelicherKriegl88
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Fr%C3%B6licher"}> Alfred Frölicher
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Kriegl"} Andreas Kriegl
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear spaces and differentiation theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.fuw.edu.pl/~kostecki/scans/froelicherkriegl1988.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and following the general conception of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensive+and+extensive"}> intensive and extensive
            ” in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere86
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              , Introduction to
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+in+Continuum+Physics"}> Categories in Continuum Physics
                , Lectures given at a Workshop held at SUNY, Buffalo 1982. Lecture Notes in Mathematics 1174. 1986
          %p
            Similar
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+theory"} sheaf theoretic
            discussion of distributions as morphisms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Paugam12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
              , section 3.2 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Towards the mathematics of quantum field theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~frederic.paugam/documents/enseignement/master-mathematical-physics.pdf"} pdf
        .revisedby
